<h2>06 — Room-by-roomThe verdict for every room in your home</h2>
<p>Generic advice fails because every room of an Indian home has different demands. Here's our specific recommendation for the seven places this question actually comes up.</p>
<h4>Modular Kitchen</h4>
<p>Laminate</p>
<p>Heat, oil, water, fingerprints, daily wear. The kitchen demands laminate for the lower cabinets, no question. Premium HPL gives 10+ years of trouble-free use at a third of the veneer cost.</p>
<h4>Master Wardrobe</h4>
<p>Hybrid</p>
<p>Laminate inside, veneer or PU on the outside shutters. The interior never shows, so spend nothing on it. The shutter face is the only thing you see — that's where the upgrade is felt.</p>
<h4>TV / Living Room Wall</h4>
<p>Veneer</p>
<p>This is the most photographed surface in your home and almost never touched. Veneer's depth and natural grain belong here. Skip laminate on this wall — guests will know.</p>
<h4>Crockery / Bar / Dining</h4>
<p>Veneer</p>
<p>Statement furniture for the dining area benefits most from veneer's warmth. A walnut or teak veneer crockery unit becomes a focal piece in a way laminate never can.</p>
<h4>Children's Bedroom</h4>
<p>Laminate</p>
<p>Crayons, stickers, footballs. Save veneer for the day they move out. A bright, durable HPL laminate is the right call here, even if your budget allows for more.</p>
<h4>Bathroom Vanity</h4>
<p>Laminate</p>
<p>Constant moisture, steam, splashes. Use water-resistant laminate over BWP plywood. Veneer in a bathroom is a slow disaster — even with PU sealing, the edges will give way.</p>
<h4>Pooja Room</h4>
<p>Veneer</p>
<p>A space that benefits from warmth and authenticity. Teak or walnut veneer on the unit and surrounding panelling gives the room the gravitas of solid wood at a fraction of the cost.</p>
<h4>Home Office / Study</h4>
<p>Hybrid</p>
<p>Veneer on the back wall or library shelving for ambience; laminate on the desk and storage drawers for daily wear. Mixes well within the same room.</p>
