<h2>07 — InsiderThe hybrid approach (what designers actually do)</h2>
<h3>The 70-30 rule we use with most clients</h3>
<p>For roughly 8 in 10 homes, the right answer is not laminate <em>or</em> veneer. It's both — strategically allocated. We call it the 70-30 split: roughly 70% of the surfaces in laminate, 30% in veneer, placed where it'll be seen and admired but rarely abused.</p>
<p>The result looks like a full-veneer home from the entrance, performs like a laminate home in daily use, and costs 40–55% less than going veneer on everything.</p>
<h3>Where to put your veneer budget</h3>
<p>If you're going to spend on veneer, spend it in these three places — in this order:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The TV / living room wall</strong> — the most photographed surface in your home. This is where guests judge your taste. Veneer here pays back in admiration for years.</li>
  <li><strong>The entry-facing kitchen wall or island</strong> — if your kitchen is visible from the living room, veneer on the upper cabinets that face the entry creates a continuous warm-wood story across both spaces.</li>
  <li><strong>The crockery / dining feature wall</strong> — a single tall veneer unit anchors the dining area and reads as a custom-built piece even when it isn't.</li>
</ol>
<h3>Where to firmly stay in laminate</h3>
<ul>
  <li>All internal carcasses (no one sees them)</li>
  <li>Children's room furniture</li>
  <li>Bathroom vanities and any wet area</li>
  <li>The lower cabinets of the kitchen (heavy daily wear)</li>
  <li>Utility storage and laundry units</li>
  <li>Any rental or short-stay property</li>
</ul>
<p>Pair this with consistent hardware (the same handle finish across both laminate and veneer units) and the join becomes invisible. Most guests cannot tell which is which.</p>
<h2>08 — Honest5 scams every homeowner falls for</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The "recon veneer sold as natural" trick.</strong> Reconstituted veneer costs 30–50% less than natural — but visually looks similar to an untrained eye. Many vendors quote natural prices and ship recon. Ask for the species name and origin in writing: "natural Burma teak" or "natural American walnut". Vague answers mean recon.</li>
  <li><strong>The "veneer ply" bundle.</strong> Vendors sometimes quote a "veneer finish kitchen" at suspiciously low rates by skipping the top coat — leaving you with raw veneer that scratches and stains within months. Always insist on PU finish or melamine top coat in the BOQ.</li>
  <li><strong>The "premium laminate" upsell.</strong> Some quotes call standard 0.8mm HPL "premium" and charge ₹250/sqft. Real premium laminate is 1mm thick, textured or metallic, from named brands. Ask for brand, thickness, and the laminate code — anything less, you're being overcharged.</li>
  <li><strong>The "matching grain" promise.</strong> Natural veneer cannot perfectly match across multiple shutters. If a vendor promises identical grain on 8 shutters of natural teak veneer, they're either selling you recon or planning to swap mid-job. Set expectations early.</li>
  <li><strong>The "lifetime warranty" claim.</strong> No interior finish has a lifetime warranty in any honest sense. Realistic warranties: 5 years on laminate, 7–10 years on veneer with proper PU finish. Anything beyond that is marketing.</li>
</ol>
<h2>09 — VerifyThe quality detection checklist</h2>
<p>Whether you've gone laminate, veneer or both, run through this checklist before signing off. These are the seven checks our quality team does on every unit before it leaves the factory.</p>
<p>01</p>
<p><strong>Brand and code stamped on the back.</strong> Genuine HPL laminate from Merino, Greenlam, Sundek, Royale Touche etc. carries a printed code on the reverse side. No code = no traceability = likely counterfeit.</p>
<p>02</p>
<p><strong>Thickness check.</strong> A 1mm HPL feels noticeably heavier and stiffer than a 0.8mm budget sheet. Carry a digital caliper if you're paranoid — most premium showrooms have one on hand.</p>
<p>03</p>
<p><strong>Veneer thickness and grain variation.</strong> A natural veneer sheet of 0.5mm shows real grain depth when viewed at an angle. A 0.3mm budget veneer looks flat and almost printed.</p>
<p>04</p>
<p><strong>Edge banding alignment.</strong> Run a fingernail along the edges of laminate shutters. A clean factory finish has no perceptible step where the edge band meets the face.</p>
<p>05</p>
<p><strong>Veneer top coat depth.</strong> A properly PU-finished veneer shutter shows a noticeable depth of clear coat over the wood — the grain looks like it's "under" the surface. Cheap top coats sit flat.</p>
<p>06</p>
<p><strong>Grain matching on adjacent panels.</strong> On veneer, adjacent shutters should have grain that flows naturally or is deliberately book-matched. Random grain on a multi-shutter wardrobe is sloppy.</p>
<p>07</p>
<p><strong>Substrate quality.</strong> Open any shutter and check the core board. Boilo-MDF or BWP plywood = good. Standard MDF on a kitchen = trouble in 3 years. Particle board on a wardrobe = even sooner.</p>
