<h2>10 — 10-year viewWhat maintenance actually looks like</h2>
<p>Lifespan claims mean nothing without context. Here's what owning each finish actually feels like across a decade.</p>
<h3>Laminate — years 1 to 10</h3>
<p>Year 1–3: looks as new on day one. Year 3–5: faint wear around handles and high-touch zones, especially on glossy finishes. Year 5–7: visible scratches on lower cabinet doors, occasional edge chipping on cheaper sheets. Year 7–10: surface still functional, but glossy finishes show micro-scratches that no polish hides. Replacement starts becoming a sensible option around year 10.</p>
<p><strong>Day-to-day care:</strong> wipe with a damp microfibre. No special polish needed. Avoid acetone, bleach, and abrasive scrubbers. Repair is mostly "replace the damaged shutter".</p>
<h3>Veneer — years 1 to 15</h3>
<p>Year 1–3: the depth and warmth that you paid for are at their peak. Year 3–6: the wood matures and darkens slightly — usually flattering. Year 6–10: the top coat starts to show micro-scratches and may lose some sheen on heavily used edges. Year 10–12: time for the first refinish — sanding the top coat back and applying a fresh PU layer. Year 12–15: still entirely usable after the refinish; the underlying veneer is unchanged.</p>
<p><strong>Day-to-day care:</strong> dust weekly with a soft cloth. Quarterly polish with a veneer-specific wax or compound. Keep out of direct sun where possible — south-facing windows will fade veneer over years. Refinishing every 8–10 years adds 5+ years of life each cycle.</p>
<h4>The 10-year cost reality</h4>
<p>Laminate kitchen at ₹150/sqft, expected replacement at year 10: total 10-year cost ≈ ₹150/sqft. Veneer kitchen at ₹500/sqft, with one ₹100/sqft refinish at year 8: total 10-year cost ≈ ₹600/sqft. Veneer is ~4x the laminate cost over a decade — not the 3x sticker difference would suggest. Worth knowing before you commit.</p>
<h2>11 — VerdictThe right call by budget tier</h2>
<p>One last lens. Whatever the room, here's how we'd advise based on the overall interior budget you're working with.</p>
<p>BudgetUnder ₹15 lakh</p>
<p><strong>Go all-laminate.</strong> Don't even consider veneer at this tier — the budget will not stretch to do veneer properly, and badly-applied veneer is worse than a great laminate. Spend the saved money on better hardware, better lighting, and BWP/HDHMR boards. Use premium HPL (1mm) for visible surfaces, standard LPL for internal carcasses.</p>
<p>Mid-Premium₹15–35 lakh</p>
<p><strong>Hybrid — the sweet spot.</strong> Laminate on the kitchen (lowers and tall units), wardrobe interiors, children's rooms, vanities. Veneer on the TV wall, crockery unit, and one statement element in the master bedroom. This is where the 70-30 rule pays off the hardest — the home reads premium without overspending.</p>
<p>Premium₹35–60 lakh</p>
<p><strong>Veneer on most surfaces, laminate strategically.</strong> Natural veneer with PU finish on living, dining, master bedroom, pooja room, study. Laminate only where it earns its keep — inside the kitchen lower cabinets, bathroom vanities, utility. Hardware moves to brushed brass or solid metal at this tier.</p>
<p>Luxury₹60 lakh+</p>
<p><strong>Veneer-led with material variety.</strong> Premium natural veneers (walnut, fumed oak, ebony) become the base, with PU finish, back-painted glass, and stone surfaces layered in for variety. Solid wood elements on dining and seating. Laminate is essentially absent except in invisible carcass interiors.</p>
